.TH LIBMPQ__UPDATE_TIMESTAMP_IDENTITY 3 2026-11-11 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__update_timestamp_identity \- stage an existing MPQ member FILETIME update
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <libmpq/mpq.h>

int32_t libmpq__update_timestamp_identity(
        mpq_update_s *update,
        const char *filename,
        uint64_t filetime,
        uint16_t locale,
        uint16_t platform);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Set existing Windows FILETIME metadata in the current successful transaction
stage. FILETIME is an unsigned count of 100-nanosecond intervals since
1601-01-01 UTC. Every uint64_t value is valid, including zero and UINT64_MAX.
Zero is an available timestamp, not an unavailable-value marker.
.PP
Select the exact filename, locale and platform without fallback. (0,0)
selects the exact neutral identity.
.PP
The target requires valid, available FILETIME metadata and usable classic
block-table provenance. This operation neither creates (attributes) nor
enables missing arrays, clears availability, or imports filesystem times.
Targets without classic attributes provenance and existing unsupported
reconstruction layouts are rejected.
.PP
FILETIME belongs to a physical classic block row. A request assigning its
current value is a validated no-op, including shared rows. It does not rewrite
the stage or invalidate signatures. Actual changes to a row with more than
one live hash identity are rejected, including unrelated unknown-name aliases.
No identity is detached and no payload is duplicated.
.PP
Supported changes preserve payload offsets, packed bytes, compression,
encryption/KEY, checksums, and unrelated attributes and identities.
Attributes and applicable structural/raw MD5 are regenerated. The archive
generation and v4 raw-chunk policy are preserved. Actual changes remove stale
weak/strong signatures under the existing update policy; they do not re-sign.
MPQE requires the MPQE transaction entry point.
.PP
Operations resolve the current stage, so the last successful timestamp wins.
Ordinary replacement resets FILETIME to zero; exact replacement retains it.
Set the timestamp after ordinary replacement when a different value is needed.
.PP
Semantic rejection preserves the preceding valid stage. Operational failure
during candidate adoption may leave the transaction abort-only. The original
archive is unchanged before successful publication. This call does not consume
the update handle; commit and abort retain their consuming behavior.
.PP
Discoverable embedded archives retain all prefix bytes and the original MPQ
origin under the existing format restrictions. Named reconstruction rejects
unrelated trailers; ordinary legacy v1 and v2 operations retain their suffix policy.
See libmpq__update_begin(3) for discovery and temporary-storage requirements.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success, including validated no-ops, or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
Missing targets or exact selectors and invalid arguments use
LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST. Missing/unavailable FILETIME, malformed attributes,
ambiguous identities, actual changes to shared rows, special-member targets,
and unsupported mappings/layouts return LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT. Allocation,
size, and I/O failures use the existing corresponding errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libmpq (3),
.BR libmpq__file_attributes (3),
.BR libmpq__writer_timestamp (3),
.BR libmpq__update_commit (3),
.BR libmpq__update_abort (3)
